# Lists

A list holds its elements in order. Each element has a position, and the same
value may appear more than once:

```hql
[412, 280, 310, 280]
```

```text
[412, 280, 310, 280]
```

The type of a list names the type of its elements:

<!-- run: check -->
```hql
[412, 280, 310, 280]
```

```text
List<Int>
```

An empty list has no elements to take a type from, so `[]` is a
`List<Never>`. A declared type gives it one: `pages : List<Int> = []`.

A list whose elements have different types is a list of `Data`, the type that
accepts any value:

<!-- run: check -->
```hql
[412, "many"]
```

```text
List<Data>
```

Such a list is valid, but the checker knows nothing about its elements. Keep
lists to one type, and use `Data` only for data from outside the program.

## Positions

```d2
pages: "pages = [412, 280, 310, 145]" {
  grid-rows: 3
  grid-columns: 4
  grid-gap: 0

  f0: "0" {style.fill: "#ffffff"; style.stroke: "#ffffff"}
  f1: "1" {style.fill: "#ffffff"; style.stroke: "#ffffff"}
  f2: "2" {style.fill: "#ffffff"; style.stroke: "#ffffff"}
  f3: "3" {style.fill: "#ffffff"; style.stroke: "#ffffff"}

  v0: "412"
  v1: "280" {style.fill: "#eef6ee"}
  v2: "310" {style.fill: "#eef6ee"}
  v3: "145"

  b0: "-4" {style.fill: "#ffffff"; style.stroke: "#ffffff"}
  b1: "-3" {style.fill: "#ffffff"; style.stroke: "#ffffff"}
  b2: "-2" {style.fill: "#ffffff"; style.stroke: "#ffffff"}
  b3: "-1" {style.fill: "#ffffff"; style.stroke: "#ffffff"}
}

note: "pages[0] is 412\npages[-1] is 145\npages[1:3] is [280, 310]\npages[9] is none" {
  shape: text
}

pages -> note {style.opacity: 0}
```

`xs[i]` reads the element at position `i`. Positions start at `0`:

```hql
pages = [412, 280, 310, 145]
pages[0]
```

```text
412
```

A negative position counts from the end, so `pages[-1]` is the last element.
A position past the end gives `none`:

```hql
pages = [412, 280, 310, 145]
[pages[-1], pages[9]]
```

```text
[145, none]
```

`xs[i]` returns an `Option<T>`, because the list may be shorter than `i`. The
program must say what a missing element means, for example with
`unwrap_or`:

```hql
pages = [412, 280, 310, 145]
pages[9].unwrap_or(0)
```

```text
0
```

`xs[a:b]` is a **slice**: the elements from position `a` up to, but not
including, position `b`. Either bound may be left out:

```hql
pages = [412, 280, 310, 145]
[pages[1:3], pages[:2], pages[2:]]
```

```text
[[280, 310], [412, 280], [310, 145]]
```

`first` and `last` read the two ends, and `take` and `drop` keep or skip the
first `n` elements:

```hql
pages = [412, 280, 310, 145]
[pages | take(2), pages | drop(2)]
```

```text
[[412, 280], [310, 145]]
```

## Joining and splitting

`concat` joins two lists. `+` does not: it adds numbers and joins strings, but
it is not defined for lists.

```hql
[412, 280] | concat([310])
```

```text
[412, 280, 310]
```

`collect(n)` splits a list into batches of `n` elements. The last batch may be
shorter. `emit` joins a list of lists back into one:

```hql
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5] | collect(2)
```

```text
[[1, 2], [3, 4], [5]]
```

```hql
[[1, 2], [3, 4], [5]] | emit
```

```text
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

## Ordering

`sort` orders the elements by their own order: numbers by value, strings
character by character, dates by time. `reverse` reverses a list:

```hql
[412, 280, 310] | sort
```

```text
[280, 310, 412]
```

```hql
[412, 280, 310] | sort | reverse
```

```text
[412, 310, 280]
```

`sort` also takes a function. A function of one element gives the key to sort
by; a function of two elements returns an `Ordering` and compares them
directly:

```hql
["Frankenstein", "Emma", "Dune"] | sort(by = t => t.size())
```

```text
["Emma", "Dune", "Frankenstein"]
```

`"Emma"` and `"Dune"` have the same length, so they keep the order they had.
A sort with this property is called **stable**.

```hql
[412, 280, 310] | sort((a, b) => compare(b, a))
```

```text
[412, 310, 280]
```

`top_k(n)` returns the `n` greatest elements, greatest first.

`min` and `max` return the least and the greatest element. An empty list has
neither, so both return an `Option`:

```hql
[412, 280, 310] | min
```

```text
280
```

```hql
[] | List<Int> | min
```

```text
none
```

`[] | List<Int>` gives the empty list its element type: it is
`List<Int>([])`, a constructor written as a step.

## Pairs

`zip` pairs the elements of two lists by position, and `enumerate` pairs each
element with its position. Both return a list of `Pair`s, and a `Pair` has
two fields, `first` and `second`:

```hql
zip(["Dune", "Emma"], [412, 474])
```

```text
[Pair(first = "Dune", second = 412), Pair(first = "Emma", second = 474)]
```

```hql
["Dune", "Emma", "Ubik"]
| enumerate
| map(p => fmt("{}. {}", p.first + 1, p.second))
```

```text
["1. Dune", "2. Emma", "3. Ubik"]
```

## Equality

Two lists are equal when they have the same elements at the same positions:

```hql
[[1, 2, 3] == [1, 2, 3], [1, 2, 3] == [3, 2, 1]]
```

```text
[true, false]
```

For a group of values where the order does not matter, use a
[[sets|set]].
